          %h5#FunctorsWithSmashProduct Functors with smash product
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric monoidal smash product of spectra
            discussed
            %a{:href => "#OnPreExcisiveFunctors"} below
            is an instance of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            (def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalDayConvolutionProduct"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            are going to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonoidsInMonoidalCategory"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+a+monoidal+category"} monoids
            with respect to Day convolution. These turn out to have a particularly transparent expression as what is called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors+with+smash+product"} functors with smash product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functors"} lax monoidal functors
            from the base monoidal category to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Top
                      %mi cg
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top^{\ast/}_{cg}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R_{n_1} \wedge R_{n_2} \longrightarrow R_{n_1 + n_2}
          %p
            satisfying suitable conditions. This is the form in which the structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            usually appears in examples. It is directly analogous to how a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+chain+complexes"} tensor product of chain complexes
            (example
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#dgAlgebraAreMonoidsInChainComplexes"}
          %p
            Here we introduce the concepts of monoidal functors and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors+with+smash+product"} functors with smash product
            and prove that they are equivalently the monoids with respect to Day convolution.
